# Real parts, no approximation

- **Geometry comes from data, never from memory.** Connector poses, track paths, footprints, gauges, switch offsets and module dimensions are imported from LDraw, LDCad or published standards by the pipeline. Never type them into code. The only literal constants allowed are the canonical units: LDU, stud 20, plate 8, brick 24, chunk 640.
- **Connector sources, in this order.** Each connector comes from the first tier that yields it:
  1. LDCad `!LDCAD SNAP_*` metadata from the pinned shadow library, shipped in its own CC BY-SA 4.0 package (see `ldraw-licensing.mdc`). Project patch files written in the same SNAP format sit in this tier too, in the same package. Their values are measured from the part's geometry, each records its provenance, and each stays `unverified` until checked.
  2. LDraw primitive references in the part's own file tree (`stud*`, stud tubes, `peghole`, `axlehole`, `connect*` and the like), read through a verified primitive table.
  3. Geometric inference measured from the part's own geometry. Its sizes are identified to the SNAP section vocabulary, or the connector is reported and never joins. It's flagged as inferred in the data and shown with a badge.

  Precedence is per exact slot (origin, gender, axis code): a lower tier only fills slots that no higher tier occupies. Only `SNAP_CLEAR` removes a connector; a commented-out snap line is a note, not a removal. A part that no tier can extract is reported, never guessed. Studio and LDD connectivity data are never used: connectors come only from the tiers above, read from the part's own geometry and the pinned data.
- **Unverified facts (§11) stay flagged.** Until verified, a value is marked `unverified` in data and its rules show "unverified" warnings. Don't guess a number to make something work.
- **Integer LDU on the placement lattice, sixteenths for connector origins.** Placement poses are integer LDU: x and z are the plan axes, and y counts whole plates above the top face of the baseplate. Connector origins inside parts are identified once, at import, to the exact 1/16-LDU grid (residual ≤ 0.0005 LDU per coordinate) and stored as integer sixteenths. Every connector key, slot and slide step uses those sixteenths, and nothing rounds them back to whole LDU. LDraw files are −Y up, so convert exactly once, at the compile and import boundary. Floats are for rendering and for imported models' internal matrices only, and never for joins.
- **Exact joins.** Track ends join if and only if their ℤ[ζ_N] coefficient vectors are equal and their headings differ by N/2. Other connectors mate only when their identified origins (in sixteenths) coincide, their axis codes are opposed, their sections fit by containment and their group tokens are equal. Models sit on the 20-LDU lattice with 90° rotations at whole plate heights. There's no snapping radius and no "close enough". The one join ε is 1e-6 LDU, and only for algebraic poses outside the field, to absorb representation error, never physical slack. Separately, a pose imported from LDraw decimals is identified with a field element only if its residual to the unique ℤ[ζ₁₆] candidate is ≤ 0.0005 LDU (§5.3; a project decision delegated by the owner). That's a one-time data-identification tolerance at import, not a join tolerance. Every 0.0005 LDU identification residual in this file is read per coordinate (L∞): each coordinate must lie within 0.0005 LDU on its own, never a Euclidean or summed distance (owner approval, 2026-11-18; the basis is half the last decimal of each printed 3-decimal coordinate). A sixteenth printed to three decimals sits exactly on that limit, so it identifies (owner decision, 2026-11-18). Part-local identification compares the parsed decimals exactly wherever they're available. Model-frame identification, whose values come from Float64 matrix products, compares against 0.0005 plus a 1e-9 LDU guard. Like the join ε, that guard only absorbs representation error and is never physical slack. Native and WASM give the same result at the limit. The same residual has one more use (owner decision, 2026-11-18): each track end's rail head bands, measured on its end face along its identified normal and closed with their image under the joint's half-turn (official heads end in a half-lap, so the face carries half of each head; seam ends are identified within the same residual), are identified to the unique measured rail in `geometry.json` `rails[]`, once at the pipeline and import boundary, and recorded per connector. Only ends whose pose is certified are identified. It's never a join or contact tolerance: wheel-on-rail contact still compares exactly with the `rails[]` decimals. A track end that identifies to no rail is reported and left out of rail-fit checks, never treated as matching one.

  The residual's uses, each explained in `docs/architecture.md` §5.3:
  1. **Pose identification:** an imported pose to its unique ℤ[ζ₁₆] candidate, and connector origins to sixteenths.
  2. **Rail heads (owner decision, 2026-11-18):** each certified track end's rail head bands to the unique measured rail.
  3. **Top-surface coverage (owner decision, 2026-11-20):** whether a cell of a part's baked `topSurface` counts as covered by the faces at its top. It moves nothing; it only decides whether the rounding slivers between printed faces count as covered.

  Official OMR model files have one more data identification, at the pipeline boundary only, behind an option the import worker never sets. Each type-1 position coordinate of a line whose own rotation is an exact signed permutation is identified once, in its parent frame, from its printed decimals in exact arithmetic.
  - It first takes the 0.0005 sixteenth step above.
  - Otherwise it identifies to whole LDU within 0.05.
  - Otherwise, if it's printed with two or more decimals, it identifies to the coarsest ½, ¼, ⅛ or 1/16 grid within its print-rounding bound: half its last printed decimal, inclusive, and never below 0.0005. A grid is used only when that bound is below half its spacing.

  At most one grid can apply, so the order doesn't matter. Moving parts relative to each other this way is accepted as dust removal and recorded per coordinate. Every other coordinate stays as authored, off-lattice and reported, never snapped or compared with any ε. User imports keep 0.0005. It's never a join tolerance: joins then compare the identified sixteenths exactly.
- **No silent substitutes.** An unresolved part is skipped and reported, never turned into a placeholder. An unknown colour is never snapped: its instances are skipped and reported. A connector that none of the three tiers can extract is an error, reported, never a guess. Never substitute logo studs.
- **Imported models are immutable.** Badges (L1, L2, provenance) never block placement and never rewrite bricks. The pipeline's one-time identification of official OMR positions (above) is data identification with provenance, not an edit; the source file is stored unchanged.
- **Validity is strict.** Layout and plan rules are zero-tolerance. The "real-world play" tolerance mode never counts as Valid. Sandbox mode changes errors into warnings. It doesn't change the geometry.
- **Third-party is namespaced and flagged.** Ids look like `ldraw:53401`. `thirdParty` provenance is refused unless `allowThirdParty` is set, and the BOM always flags it.
- Never weaken a rule, widen a tolerance or edit a golden file just to make a test pass. Find the real cause or ask.
